                        "entry_id": "af619f14-0c87-36a0-8793-760e7b062a48",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A viviparous female aphid that is born in the autumn on a secondary host, then flies to the primary host where she produces new females that mate and deposit eggs."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "084a4b8c-287b-4282-9eef-e5e5e166c184",
                                "sense_id": "899f490f-4613-3e93-8b6f-42f6dd526a14",
                                "text": "Field experience has shown that the arrival of a single gynopara (and one or more males) is sufficient to produce a severe infestation over the entire tree the following spring."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "05f049b7-2fcd-4d4e-b49e-cda4cbf7dbad",
                                "sense_id": "899f490f-4613-3e93-8b6f-42f6dd526a14",
                                "text": "And in the absence of evidence to the contrary, one must conclude that the gynopara constitutes a distinct form in these species."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "35fc9f08-9f2d-4f3f-8640-c824c78d6623",
                                "sense_id": "899f490f-4613-3e93-8b6f-42f6dd526a14",
                                "text": "Sex allocation by dimorphic host-alternating species, however, can best be described as sequential hermaphroditism: gynoparae are produced before males (Figure 5.5; Dixon and Glen, 1971)."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
